00:08:35.529 --> 00:08:36.949
John Talburt: R.

84
00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:39.889
John Talburt: We won't learn all about quality, but…

85
00:08:40.159 --> 00:08:42.399
John Talburt: a couple of things. One is that

86
00:08:42.809 --> 00:08:47.859
John Talburt: To have any process that you consider to be a quality process.

87
00:08:48.379 --> 00:08:55.829
John Talburt: Then you… you need to recognize some things. One is, and I think we mentioned this, or I mentioned this before.

88
00:08:56.289 --> 00:09:04.199
John Talburt: is the difference between quality assurance and quality control, and we'll see that as we talk about some of these people.

89
00:09:04.799 --> 00:09:12.249
John Talburt: But it all comes around to this idea that what we're really doing is building information products and services.

90
00:09:12.869 --> 00:09:19.759
John Talburt: So, in the manufacturing process, Quality control is the… the check.

91
00:09:19.909 --> 00:09:31.959
John Talburt: against the product that you've built. If you're building a car, it would be quality control comes at the point that you've actually completely manufactured the car, and you're ready to ship it.

92
00:09:32.099 --> 00:09:38.259
John Talburt: So before it's shipped, you have certain standards, you have certain requirements.

93
00:09:38.399 --> 00:09:41.239
John Talburt: That that product or that service has to meet.

94
00:09:41.339 --> 00:09:45.259
John Talburt: And if it meets those, then it passes quality control.

95
00:09:45.939 --> 00:09:50.189
John Talburt: And I think I also mentioned earlier that we've kind of gone through

96
00:09:50.339 --> 00:09:53.159
John Talburt: some cycles here in the United States.

97
00:09:53.589 --> 00:10:00.519
John Talburt: Between quality control Which is inspecting the final product.

98
00:10:00.679 --> 00:10:03.849
John Talburt: And then the other side of that is quality assurance.

99
00:10:04.239 --> 00:10:11.159
John Talburt: And quality assurance are those things that you do during the building process, during the manufacturing process.

100
00:10:11.549 --> 00:10:14.569
John Talburt: To check, to be sure that

101
00:10:14.829 --> 00:10:21.409
John Talburt: That the final product or the final service that you build will meet quality control standards.

102
00:10:21.859 --> 00:10:26.109
John Talburt: And those things that you do during the process

103
00:10:26.259 --> 00:10:32.059
John Talburt: are called quality assurance. You're trying to assure that you will meet quality control.

104
00:10:32.879 --> 00:10:36.079
John Talburt: So, that's a couple of things. The other is that…

105
00:10:36.309 --> 00:10:42.989
John Talburt: All of the failures of quality assurance and quality control are recorded

106
00:10:43.439 --> 00:10:59.919
John Talburt: or should be recorded, and then those should be analyzed to try to continually improve your process to eliminate those sources of problems that you detect through quality assurance and quality control. So those are two very essential parts of any

107
00:11:00.099 --> 00:11:01.639
John Talburt: quality process.

108
00:11:01.969 --> 00:11:06.439
John Talburt: So, anyway, back to, to, shoehardt.

109
00:11:08.139 --> 00:11:17.039
John Talburt: His idea was that you need to constantly check your product, your quality control.

110
00:11:17.479 --> 00:11:23.879
John Talburt: And so, it turns out, then, that it depends on what you're building, right?

111
00:11:24.289 --> 00:11:31.319
John Talburt: So, in many cases, you can't inspect every product that you build.

112
00:11:31.819 --> 00:11:37.579
John Talburt: For a car, you probably can. I mean, even though they build hundreds of cars a day.

113
00:11:37.729 --> 00:11:40.449
John Talburt: It's practical to go in and

114
00:11:40.859 --> 00:11:46.809
John Talburt: Inspect every one of them to be sure that it meets the quality control standards that you have.

115
00:11:47.199 --> 00:11:50.439
John Talburt: But for other things, that…

116
00:11:50.779 --> 00:11:55.109
John Talburt: It can prove to be very difficult, or in some cases, impossible.

117
00:11:55.779 --> 00:12:02.249
John Talburt: So, in the case, for example, suppose you're building, hairpins.

118
00:12:02.609 --> 00:12:08.169
John Talburt: You know, and you're gonna produce millions of these hairpins, or hundreds of thousands of these a day.

119
00:12:08.459 --> 00:12:14.079
John Talburt: But you want to be sure they're good, they're correct, and they're doing what they're supposed to do.

120
00:12:14.409 --> 00:12:20.939
John Talburt: And so in that case, what you would do is you would sample those. You couldn't… you couldn't expect every one of them.

121
00:12:21.049 --> 00:12:28.479
John Talburt: So you would have a way of sampling the outputs, all the hairpins you were building, And,

122
00:12:29.019 --> 00:12:38.329
John Talburt: And then you would base it on that, and that's… that's what statistical process control… we call it statistical. We do sampling.

123
00:12:38.989 --> 00:12:44.429
John Talburt: The other place where it'd be impossible if you're building bonbons or some kind of candy.

124
00:12:44.649 --> 00:12:48.729
John Talburt: Right? You're manufacturing, some kind of food.

125
00:12:48.909 --> 00:12:54.379
John Talburt: You wouldn't be able to… Taste every one of them, or you wouldn't have anything to sell, right?

126
00:12:54.769 --> 00:13:01.049
John Talburt: So, so anyway, this is the reason, then, for statistical process control.

127
00:13:01.349 --> 00:13:06.699
John Talburt: And he pioneered the application of statistics, like sampling and other things.

128
00:13:06.799 --> 00:13:08.689
John Talburt: To the idea of quality.

129
00:13:09.619 --> 00:13:11.069
John Talburt: And,

130
00:13:11.629 --> 00:13:19.119
John Talburt: And some of the principles he establishes that data have no meaning apart from their context, so it only becomes information

131
00:13:19.429 --> 00:13:24.589
John Talburt: When data is put into the proper context,

132
00:13:24.729 --> 00:13:29.779
John Talburt: The other thing, and this became quite important, especially for some others that we'll talk about.

133
00:13:30.229 --> 00:13:37.149
John Talburt: That data contain both signals and noise, and so this is the beginning of this idea that you can have

134
00:13:37.259 --> 00:13:44.239
John Talburt: poor quality data. There's things in the data that you don't want, something that's wrong, anomalies.

135
00:13:44.369 --> 00:13:54.039
John Talburt: And here, he was, again, looking at it from a physicist's standpoint, and he was saying, well, it's signal and noise. In other words, it's good data, and

136
00:13:54.519 --> 00:13:55.769
John Talburt: problem data.

137
00:13:56.269 --> 00:14:02.849
John Talburt: And you have to separate those, and so this is sort of the beginning of the idea of data quality control.

138
00:14:05.019 --> 00:14:10.389
John Talburt: And then, this is something interesting, because this gets misattributed a lot.

139
00:14:10.719 --> 00:14:18.259
John Talburt: He invented the idea of plan, do, check, act cycle, this repeated cycle, PDCA.

140
00:14:18.599 --> 00:14:31.049
John Talburt: Unfortunately, most people believe that dimming, that we'll talk about later, invented this, but he didn't. It was Shuhart, and he introduced it to dimming. Deming and Shuhart worked together quite a bit.

141
00:14:31.619 --> 00:14:37.849
John Talburt: So the PDCA cycle really belongs to, or was invented by, Shuhart.

142
00:14:38.649 --> 00:14:45.319
John Talburt: So, again, statistical process control is a very simplified thing. We'll talk more about it later.

143
00:14:45.639 --> 00:14:51.139
John Talburt: is that it's a time thing, right? When you're building something and manufacturing something.

144
00:14:51.299 --> 00:14:54.319
John Talburt: You're repeatedly building it over time.

145
00:14:54.549 --> 00:15:03.369
John Talburt: And you have, you're going to be sampling and looking for things that are, out of…

146
00:15:03.539 --> 00:15:18.669
John Talburt: out of range or out of order anomalies that you don't want. And so, the idea is that over time, you can see time moving from left to right in this chart. You take a sample and you look and see if there are defects, right?

147
00:15:18.979 --> 00:15:20.539
John Talburt: And so,

148
00:15:20.939 --> 00:15:30.499
John Talburt: there's certain, what we call, tolerance, right? You… you can't stop the production line just because some things are defective. You want to try to remove them, but…

149
00:15:30.789 --> 00:15:33.259
John Talburt: You're looking for defects.

150
00:15:33.629 --> 00:15:38.279
John Talburt: And these, this graph represents things that

151
00:15:38.519 --> 00:15:43.779
John Talburt: The samples have certain levels of defects in them.

152
00:15:44.239 --> 00:15:50.199
John Talburt: But with… if they stay within what are called the control limits, upper and lower control limits.

153
00:15:50.389 --> 00:15:53.069
John Talburt: Then it's… it makes sense.

154
00:15:53.209 --> 00:16:02.549
John Talburt: And you, you, you take… you look at that, and you say, well, yeah, there's gonna be some natural variation, some normal variation.

155
00:16:02.669 --> 00:16:12.429
John Talburt: In any manufacturing process, Machines are gonna wear, and they're gonna make some… mistakes.

156
00:16:12.539 --> 00:16:18.099
John Talburt: As they… as the machines get older, or you have different operators, and so forth.

157
00:16:18.209 --> 00:16:26.969
John Talburt: But as long as you stay within the limits… so what you're trying to look for in statistical process control is when a process goes out of control.

158
00:16:27.119 --> 00:16:29.999
John Talburt: When you start seeing continual products.

159
00:16:30.119 --> 00:16:36.689
John Talburt: Or anomalies, and they start exceeding your limits, either your lower limits or your upper limits.

160
00:16:36.909 --> 00:16:41.279
John Talburt: And so, this is the idea of statistical process control.

161
00:16:42.009 --> 00:16:44.839
John Talburt: And that's Shuhart's big contribution.

162
00:16:45.109 --> 00:16:46.269
John Talburt: And,

163
00:16:47.759 --> 00:16:56.559
John Talburt: So again, he worked closely with Edward Deming, who's perhaps one of the more famous people in the area of quality, especially manufacturing.

164
00:16:57.319 --> 00:17:02.599
John Talburt: And again, he was a statistician, but he was also a professor and an engineer as well.

165
00:17:04.989 --> 00:17:12.219
John Talburt: He's a lot more philosophical than you are. He came up with what he called a system of profound knowledge.

166
00:17:13.619 --> 00:17:19.659
John Talburt: And he was a big, on the list.

167
00:17:19.889 --> 00:17:29.039
John Talburt: He came up with 14 points for organizational transformation, how to transform organizations. So, in some ways, not only was he father of

168
00:17:29.259 --> 00:17:30.539
John Talburt: quality.

169
00:17:30.759 --> 00:17:45.719
John Talburt: But, there's also sort of the beginning of what we call change management, which is another course that we'll be offering in the spring that talks about how you transform organizations to stay current.

170
00:17:46.169 --> 00:17:52.189
John Talburt: And then, perhaps the thing that he's most famous for

