00:19:02.309 --> 00:19:04.949
John Talburt: Good quality management.

181
00:19:06.119 --> 00:19:11.589
John Talburt: And sort of the key message that Deming has is that,

182
00:19:12.409 --> 00:19:15.529
John Talburt: He was coming from a time in manufacturing.

183
00:19:15.759 --> 00:19:19.819
John Talburt: Where manufacturers, if there were problems with their products.

184
00:19:20.099 --> 00:19:26.289
John Talburt: always tended to blame the workers. Oh, you know, somebody messed up on the assembly line, or whatever.

185
00:19:26.529 --> 00:19:30.609
John Talburt: And Deming's message was that they had it all backwards.

186
00:19:30.739 --> 00:19:34.069
John Talburt: That these problems, and the cause of these problems.

187
00:19:34.179 --> 00:19:46.369
John Talburt: were mostly in the management of the process, that management was 90% responsible, and only 10% of the problems were actually coming from the workers. So he really turned that on its head.

188
00:19:46.789 --> 00:19:51.629
John Talburt: And, he came up with the seven deadly sins. Again, probably have…

189
00:19:51.779 --> 00:19:54.639
John Talburt: His most famous contribution to quality.

190
00:19:55.089 --> 00:20:02.769
John Talburt: And so the first one is the lack of constancy of purpose.

191
00:20:02.949 --> 00:20:12.429
John Talburt: In other words, the idea that when you're making something, you're manufacturing, you keep changing your target, you keep moving and,

192
00:20:12.699 --> 00:20:18.169
John Talburt: It's certainly true that you need to manage the life cycle of a product, and not

193
00:20:18.469 --> 00:20:31.049
John Talburt: keep making the same thing over and over, even when it's not popular or selling. But at the same time, you… you can't just keep changing the direction that you're going constantly.

194
00:20:31.089 --> 00:20:42.129
John Talburt: Cause this really, causes the workers to lose attention and become confused about what's the real mission, that you're under.

195
00:20:42.219 --> 00:20:49.929
John Talburt: So, when you see these sorts of changes, he says that's a symptom of a problem that's going to result in poor quality.

196
00:20:50.689 --> 00:20:57.729
John Talburt: And this one, I wish people would, still… Think about this one.

197
00:20:58.059 --> 00:21:01.549
John Talburt: And that's emphasis on short-term profits.

198
00:21:01.749 --> 00:21:08.129
John Talburt: And that's certainly a huge problem that we have today, because now, in our current financial situation.

199
00:21:08.329 --> 00:21:16.719
John Talburt: The way, the observer's work is that now everything is on a quarterly basis, right?

200
00:21:17.219 --> 00:21:21.999
John Talburt: Instead of thinking about the year, two years, things that ahead.

201
00:21:22.299 --> 00:21:24.969
John Talburt: And what's going to be the long-term benefit?

202
00:21:25.799 --> 00:21:31.789
John Talburt: The financial analysts are always on everybody's case at the end of a quarter.

203
00:21:32.039 --> 00:21:40.969
John Talburt: And they try to project what you should be making, what kind of profits you have each quarter, rather than thinking of the long-term investment.

204
00:21:41.499 --> 00:21:44.919
John Talburt: But anyway, he said that was a symptom of poor quality, is

205
00:21:45.179 --> 00:21:53.979
John Talburt: Constantly looking at short-term, somewhat like constancy of purpose, where you're changing your focus, short term.

206
00:21:56.889 --> 00:22:10.569
John Talburt: Another is performance appraisals that promote fear. And again, this is still a bit of a problem, I think, in modern corporations, but not as bad as it was in the old days.

207
00:22:10.839 --> 00:22:12.839
John Talburt: And this is the idea that…

208
00:22:14.179 --> 00:22:33.519
John Talburt: you know, we're watching you and your performance, and you have to meet certain standards or quotas, or, you know, you're going to get fired, you're going to get demoted. Instead of trying to encourage workers, you tried to kind of motivate them by fear of losing their job.

209
00:22:34.589 --> 00:22:42.799
John Talburt: The other, and again, some of these still, are important today.

210
00:22:43.119 --> 00:22:45.809
John Talburt: This idea of mobility of management.

211
00:22:46.649 --> 00:22:50.259
John Talburt: And we still sort of have this idea

212
00:22:50.729 --> 00:22:57.429
John Talburt: For example, now, even here at the university, we confer a degree called A degree in management.

213
00:22:57.829 --> 00:23:01.939
John Talburt: And it's the idea that once you learn the principles of management.

214
00:23:02.149 --> 00:23:04.429
John Talburt: You can sort of manage anything.

215
00:23:05.059 --> 00:23:11.029
John Talburt: And so, what he was arguing against here in his day in the manufacturing processes.

216
00:23:11.489 --> 00:23:14.879
John Talburt: They often had this idea of rotating management.

217
00:23:15.269 --> 00:23:17.309
John Talburt: And so the guy who was,

218
00:23:17.709 --> 00:23:21.509
John Talburt: Managing the painting of the product.

219
00:23:21.809 --> 00:23:35.209
John Talburt: would periodically shift, and now he would be managing inventory or some other part of the company. The idea that… that you, you know, management is management, and you didn't have to learn the particulars in a particular context.

220
00:23:35.859 --> 00:23:37.559
John Talburt: He said that was a problem.

221
00:23:38.469 --> 00:23:43.839
John Talburt: And then, management by the use of visible numbers, or quotas.

222
00:23:44.189 --> 00:23:50.299
John Talburt: And this… this is, again, we see this a lot today.

223
00:23:50.579 --> 00:24:01.279
John Talburt: the idea that, you know, you're judging your employees by certain quotas of what they're able to do. This happens not only in manufacturing, but in sales and many other things.

224
00:24:01.739 --> 00:24:05.839
John Talburt: And also, it's very important to note, he said it's by visible numbers, where

225
00:24:06.049 --> 00:24:11.689
John Talburt: You know, I've seen, you know, like, when they do fundraising, and they put a big thermometer on the wall.

226
00:24:11.979 --> 00:24:16.319
John Talburt: So, you know, we've reached this level of contribution, or that level.

227
00:24:16.529 --> 00:24:18.799
John Talburt: And making it visible to everyone.

228
00:24:19.479 --> 00:24:22.739
John Talburt: And he said that's not a good way to manage.

229
00:24:23.029 --> 00:24:29.219
John Talburt: Also, if you have… and again, this has to do mostly with the manufacturing, where people worked on a shop floor.

230
00:24:29.599 --> 00:24:32.019
John Talburt: That there are excessive medical costs.

231
00:24:32.139 --> 00:24:43.169
John Talburt: That's an indicator that things not only were not saved, but that the processes were not being run properly, and that could result in poor quality.

232
00:24:43.639 --> 00:24:52.429
John Talburt: And also, excessive cost of liability. If you have liability for Producing something that causes problem

233
00:24:52.919 --> 00:24:56.359
John Talburt: We see this all the time in recalls, right? Where…

234
00:24:56.679 --> 00:25:03.199
John Talburt: Something is manufactured, and it turns out to be… have some unforeseen

235
00:25:03.439 --> 00:25:07.189
John Talburt: Problem when it gets out into the public and they start using it.

236
00:25:07.339 --> 00:25:18.649
John Talburt: So, excessive cost of liability. So, he said these are the main things that you should look for to see if companies were really functioning and producing quality products or not.

237
00:25:22.459 --> 00:25:28.909
John Talburt: So, and again, the big issue here is this idea of continuous improvement.

238
00:25:29.669 --> 00:25:34.759
John Talburt: So again, if you look back at the Plan, Do, Check Act, it is a,

239
00:25:36.129 --> 00:25:41.849
John Talburt: a cycle. It's not something that just happens once. It's a cycle, and…

240
00:25:42.239 --> 00:25:48.489
John Talburt: So, you plan to do something, you do it, and then you see if you actually were able to do it correctly.

241
00:25:48.849 --> 00:26:00.889
John Talburt: And if you weren't able to do that, then you act on that to improve it, and improve your plan, and do it differently, so that this results in a continuous improvement.

242
00:26:03.719 --> 00:26:08.529
John Talburt: So, Denning had a lot of contribution, again, still very… considered very important today.

243
00:26:09.609 --> 00:26:14.099
John Talburt: And by the way, again, the Japanese contributed a lot to this.

244
00:26:15.839 --> 00:26:28.619
John Talburt: And we still use today a lot of words from Japan, because they… they were so enthusiastic about adopting these things, and they had their own, of course, language for describing them.

245
00:26:29.089 --> 00:26:34.639
John Talburt: And so, this… this idea of continuous improvement It's called Kaizen.

246
00:26:35.519 --> 00:26:41.379
John Talburt: Which in Japanese means change for the better. In other words, you keep… keep making it better.

247
00:26:44.479 --> 00:26:51.579
John Talburt: Another very important, pioneer of quality was a guy named Joseph Duran.

248
00:26:53.979 --> 00:26:58.459
John Talburt: And he's most famous for what's called the Quality Trilogy.

249
00:26:59.429 --> 00:27:03.449
John Talburt: Now, it's interesting that it's not… it's sort of a…

250
00:27:04.159 --> 00:27:13.189
John Talburt: the Plan, Do, Check, Act without one of the the, four parts, Because it's a trilogy.

251
00:27:13.629 --> 00:27:20.029
John Talburt: And, but what he's famous for is, he says that quality comes

252
00:27:20.149 --> 00:27:27.739
John Talburt: from these three things. It comes from planning, Improvement, which, sort of…

253
00:27:27.959 --> 00:27:37.069
John Talburt: sort of, you know, automatically includes the idea of study. You can't… you can't really improve something if you don't study and understand what you need to improve.

254
00:27:37.219 --> 00:27:38.749
John Talburt: And then control.

255
00:27:39.189 --> 00:27:50.039
John Talburt: And so, he's very famous for this idea of quality control, and he made the statement the…

256
00:27:50.179 --> 00:27:54.189
John Talburt: You know, you can tell quality

257
00:27:55.009 --> 00:27:57.969
John Talburt: If something… if you build something that

258
00:27:58.099 --> 00:28:01.249
John Talburt: It's fit for purpose, or fit for use.

259
00:28:01.819 --> 00:28:11.559
John Talburt: And so, he's sort of the source of… some people use this definition, and they say, well, what is data quality? They say, well, it's data that's fit for use.

260
00:28:11.769 --> 00:28:15.499
John Talburt: And this comes, really, from Duran in one of his books.

261
00:28:15.879 --> 00:28:22.159
John Talburt: But again, as we said earlier in the course,

262
00:28:23.769 --> 00:28:28.949
John Talburt: We tend to separate the concepts of data quality and information quality.

263
00:28:29.219 --> 00:28:34.039
John Talburt: Certainly, data quality is meeting, data requirements.

264
00:28:34.419 --> 00:28:37.019
John Talburt: That's the definition of policy in ISO.

265
00:28:37.119 --> 00:28:47.989
John Talburt: is… quality is meeting requirements. So, data quality is meeting data requirements. But just because data meets the requirements, you've got good data, as they say.

266
00:28:48.209 --> 00:28:57.249
John Talburt: you put it into a process, doesn't mean that you will get a quality product out of it. You'll get a product, a quality information product.

267
00:28:57.499 --> 00:29:16.289
John Talburt: there has to be a process, and it has to be planned, you have to continually improve that process, and you're really focusing on the quality control. That is, after you build it, is the thing you built have quality? Does it meet requirements of the users of that?

268
00:29:16.539 --> 00:29:29.769
John Talburt: So, you're looking at both ends of this process. In the raw product ingestion, when you're bringing data in, you want data to be good, you want it to be clean, you want it to meet requirements.

269
00:29:29.909 --> 00:29:39.169
John Talburt: But you still have to check at the end and manage it as a product. In other words, you're taking that data, you're putting it through processes.

270
00:29:39.439 --> 00:29:46.409
John Talburt: And you're coming out with an information, product, or service, then you have to check that as well.

271
00:29:46.579 --> 00:29:54.669
John Talburt: And so there's an… but they're different kinds of requirements, right? They're not requirements like… Coverage and,

272
00:29:55.019 --> 00:30:13.419
John Talburt: consistency and those sorts of things. Now you're talking about the requirements of the user. Is that… is it really what they want? Is it doing what they want it to do? So you have different types of requirements. So it's still quality in the sense of meeting requirements, but they're different kinds of requirements.

273
00:30:13.799 --> 00:30:26.649
John Talburt: On the data quality side, on the left-hand side, as data comes in, the requirements have to do with characteristics of the data. The amount of data, the manipulability of the data.

274
00:30:26.969 --> 00:30:29.969
John Talburt: The, the relevance of the data.

275
00:30:30.079 --> 00:30:32.619
John Talburt: The accuracy of the data.

276
00:30:32.769 --> 00:30:37.349
John Talburt: And you're looking at that data in terms of those data quality dimensions.

277
00:30:37.619 --> 00:30:43.379
John Talburt: But then, when you run it through the process, and you produce an information product or service.

278
00:30:43.449 --> 00:30:58.359
John Talburt: You have different kinds of requirements there. Those are the value requirements. How valuable is what you've built in terms of, is it producing value for the organization, and is it producing value for the users of that product or service?

279
00:30:58.879 --> 00:31:02.419
John Talburt: And so then it becomes more of a value proposition.

280
00:31:02.619 --> 00:31:05.519
John Talburt: So, we'll continue to talk about this idea.

281
00:31:07.919 --> 00:31:18.069
John Talburt: Okay, so… He was also big on quality planning. You needed to ask these W questions, so…

282
00:31:18.199 --> 00:31:23.559
John Talburt: Who, you know, what and how. Who are your customers?

283
00:31:24.099 --> 00:31:27.219
John Talburt: What do they want?

284
00:31:27.359 --> 00:31:30.649
John Talburt: What are their quality characteristics?

285
00:31:31.009 --> 00:31:33.359
John Talburt: So their quality characteristics

286
00:31:33.519 --> 00:31:40.719
John Talburt: May not be the ones that we think of when we're ingesting data and using data quality requirements.

287
00:31:40.979 --> 00:31:57.769
John Talburt: their characteristics. They're buying a car, they want to know, how does it handle? Does it run smoothly? Is the interior quiet? All kinds of things that… that are not part of the parts that go into the

288
00:31:58.069 --> 00:32:06.249
John Talburt: the building of the car. I mean, certainly there's a connection there, but there are different types of requirements. When you're building the car.

289
00:32:06.599 --> 00:32:18.709
John Talburt: you're interested in that the parts fit together, the tolerances in terms of the diameters and the tensile strength, and so forth. You're looking at those intrinsic qualities.

290
00:32:18.839 --> 00:32:28.199
John Talburt: But after you build the car, build that product, then it's a different set of requirements, but those are the requirements of the consumer of that product.

291
00:32:28.349 --> 00:32:30.639
John Talburt: And the same thing is true for data.

292
00:32:31.039 --> 00:32:43.559
John Talburt: So you're building an information product, like we talked earlier, you're building, here at the school, they'll build your transcript, right, or they'll build your, your bill.

293
00:32:43.559 --> 00:32:53.359
John Talburt: Right? Your fees and those sort of things. They've put together all this information to build that product that's, useful and for you.

294
00:32:54.309 --> 00:32:56.789
John Talburt: So, and then how…

295
00:32:57.299 --> 00:33:04.829
John Talburt: How do you make it, how you'll deliver it, all the things that go into the product service itself.

296
00:33:07.849 --> 00:33:12.879
John Talburt: So again, this is sort of his contribution, but again, I see this…

297
00:33:13.189 --> 00:33:15.849
John Talburt: Is one of the places where,

298
00:33:16.269 --> 00:33:27.109
John Talburt: people have kind of misapplied what Joseph… what Joanne was saying when they say, you know, well, what is data quality? Well, it's data that's fit for use.

299
00:33:27.389 --> 00:33:41.539
John Talburt: And that's, it's okay, but what he was talking about was the product, not the data, or not the parts that go in. He was talking about quality control. He was saying, you know.

300
00:33:41.709 --> 00:33:51.019
John Talburt: The quality is when you build something that people want and use, and it fits their… it fits their purpose, or what they want to use it for.

301
00:33:51.339 --> 00:34:06.469
John Talburt: If it's a car, it pulls a trailer, or it moves dirt, or it's quiet, or it saves gas, or it's powerful. Those are the kinds of things, not the kinds of things we think about when we think about

302
00:34:06.789 --> 00:34:14.119
John Talburt: the… the input side, where we're inputting the materials and the parts. We… so…

303
00:34:14.239 --> 00:34:18.719
John Talburt: We have… there are different quality characteristics that we're looking for.

304
00:34:19.499 --> 00:34:22.409
John Talburt: But nevertheless, I see that all the time still.

305
00:34:23.609 --> 00:34:36.939
John Talburt: So, his big emphasis was on quality control. Again, quality control is at the end, after you've built what you're building, whether it's a data product, I'm sorry, an information product.

306
00:34:37.029 --> 00:34:47.349
John Talburt: or a manufactured product. Quality control is checking that the final product is meeting your… the requirements of the user.

307
00:34:48.319 --> 00:34:53.929
John Talburt: So, and his idea was that this is the way you should start, and

308
00:34:54.129 --> 00:35:02.649
John Talburt: And again, this… I don't know why, when we… we moved all of these principles over, to,

309
00:35:03.179 --> 00:35:21.129
John Talburt: to information quality, it took us so long to kind of realize what he was talking about here, and that is, you… the way you manage information quality, or manage information, is as a product. You need to really think of it as a product, and this is exactly what he's saying here.

310
00:35:21.219 --> 00:35:28.509
John Talburt: Quality control, in Duran's view, is the governance process that makes sure things don't get worse.

311
00:35:28.599 --> 00:35:41.709
John Talburt: In other words, you really need to focus on the end product that you're building, whether it's an information product or a manufactured product. That's what tells you whether or not you're on the right track or not.

312
00:35:43.529 --> 00:35:56.849
John Talburt: And of course, that's inspection of actual performance against planned performance. The way you intended the product to work, or the information product to do, does… is it really meeting that?

313
00:35:57.359 --> 00:36:06.939
John Talburt: And of course, if there are differences, then that calls for an analysis we call a root cause investigation, or root cause analysis.

314
00:36:07.529 --> 00:36:14.749
John Talburt: And then again, he's very much on the camp of Shuhart and Deming to say that,

315
00:36:15.269 --> 00:36:30.879
John Talburt: You know, in many cases, you have to use statistical measures of performance to ensure control, because in many cases, it's impractical to inspect each product individually. So, often you have to come to some kind of

316
00:36:31.029 --> 00:36:33.599
John Talburt: sampling method.

317
00:36:34.549 --> 00:36:40.789
John Talburt: And so, quality improvement is focused on fitness for use and error reduction.

318
00:36:40.949 --> 00:36:47.559
John Talburt: Right, so again, he's talking about when he says, fitness for youth, or Fitness for Purpose, or…

319
00:36:47.939 --> 00:36:53.489
John Talburt: the terms that people use. He's talking about the product itself.

320
00:36:55.929 --> 00:37:01.369
John Talburt: And you want to keep improving that, this idea of continuous improvement.

321
00:37:02.079 --> 00:37:05.919
John Talburt: He also advocated a thing called breakdown thinking.

322
00:37:06.579 --> 00:37:14.089
John Talburt: to change the system and improve performance, and what he meant by… I'm sorry, not breakthrough, breakthrough thinking.

323
00:37:14.419 --> 00:37:19.969
John Talburt: Again, he thought that even though you wanted continuous improvement.

324
00:37:20.469 --> 00:37:25.609
John Talburt: He wanted people to think about, you know, how… what would be a radical change?

325
00:37:25.769 --> 00:37:29.049
John Talburt: that can improve things? What's a breakthrough thinking?

326
00:37:29.409 --> 00:37:37.729
John Talburt: That could, you know, let's just think about how would we… could we completely change what we're doing to make things even better?

327
00:37:38.019 --> 00:37:41.959
John Talburt: So, again, even though he was into this idea of

328
00:37:42.579 --> 00:37:48.999
John Talburt: quality improvement, and the idea that, Deming and others had talked about continuous improvement.

329
00:37:49.459 --> 00:37:54.439
John Talburt: It's sort of like now the modern view of evolution, you know, I mean…

330
00:37:54.579 --> 00:38:00.249
John Talburt: Darwin's initial idea is that it was a very, very slow change over generations.

331
00:38:00.579 --> 00:38:10.469
John Talburt: But, there's also, introduced the same thing, that there are certain things, there's certain radical quick changes

332
00:38:10.999 --> 00:38:16.559
John Talburt: For example, the… asteroid that hit the Earth in… Completely changed the…

333
00:38:16.719 --> 00:38:20.399
John Talburt: The evolution of dinosaurs and reptiles.

334
00:38:20.559 --> 00:38:22.529
John Talburt: Shifted over to mammals.

335
00:38:22.739 --> 00:38:36.359
John Talburt: That, that you have to think about both. You have to think about continually improving the process that you have, but be open-minded to think, is there a new way, a big change in the process that could really make things better?

336
00:38:38.909 --> 00:38:41.249
John Talburt: So Duran had a lot to contribute.

337
00:38:41.849 --> 00:38:45.809
John Talburt: And then we get into the more modern, era.

338
00:38:46.459 --> 00:38:50.919
John Talburt: And a guy named Armand Feigenbaum

339
00:38:51.969 --> 00:38:54.859
John Talburt: And he… he was, by this time.

340
00:38:55.279 --> 00:38:58.639
John Talburt: Quality control and become a profession.

341
00:38:58.759 --> 00:39:03.539
John Talburt: And he was a quality control person. That was his occupation expert.

342
00:39:04.599 --> 00:39:06.569
John Talburt: And, he…

343
00:39:06.849 --> 00:39:16.069
John Talburt: is the one who's responsible for this term TQM, or Total Quality Control, or Total Quality Management.

344
00:39:16.799 --> 00:39:22.319
John Talburt: So, and the idea here, the total means

345
00:39:22.469 --> 00:39:26.419
John Talburt: That everyone in the process is thinking about

346
00:39:26.719 --> 00:39:32.339
John Talburt: The end product, and is it going to meet the consumer or the customer's need?

347
00:39:34.059 --> 00:39:42.979
John Talburt: So even the people that are sitting in the plant, they're building a car, they need to be thinking about what they're doing as contributing.

348
00:39:43.199 --> 00:39:54.329
John Talburt: To the end product, and that everyone is thinking, everyone in the process, they're not just thinking about putting this bolt on that they have to do 100 times every day.

349
00:39:54.499 --> 00:40:02.489
John Talburt: That what that contributes to in terms of the overall quality of the product, in terms of what customers want.

350
00:40:03.849 --> 00:40:09.409
John Talburt: He also, came up with the idea.

351
00:40:09.839 --> 00:40:13.149
John Talburt: Excuse me, what was called a hidden plan.

352
00:40:15.879 --> 00:40:21.679
John Talburt: And I think all of you have seen this at one time or another. When you have some kind of process.

353
00:40:22.059 --> 00:40:26.969
John Talburt: Where you're building something, or you're building, or you're performing some kind of service.

354
00:40:27.589 --> 00:40:34.429
John Talburt: That you have planned out a nice process that's supposed to work.

355
00:40:34.709 --> 00:40:38.659
John Talburt: But when it starts in certain places where it's not working.

356
00:40:39.209 --> 00:40:46.449
John Talburt: Instead of fixing that process at that point to make it work, We work around it.

357
00:40:46.799 --> 00:40:50.839
John Talburt: In other words, we get to, you know, we produce a report.

358
00:40:51.089 --> 00:41:00.189
John Talburt: And that report should be… have controls on it, so that we should just be able to take that report and deliver it to the user of that report.

359
00:41:00.409 --> 00:41:08.309
John Talburt: But we know that in the process of building that report, there are certain things that are just not working properly. It's not doing exactly what

360
00:41:08.419 --> 00:41:24.999
John Talburt: the people that are using the report want, so you have to stop, intercept the report, and instead of giving it directly to the user, you have to say, okay, let's go in and check it. There's some things wrong here, so we have to go in and manually change some things and fix some things.

361
00:41:25.049 --> 00:41:29.639
John Talburt: Before we actually get the product that we want to deliver to the customer.

362
00:41:29.889 --> 00:41:32.179
John Talburt: And so he calls this a hidden plant.

363
00:41:32.479 --> 00:41:38.509
John Talburt: It's this extra work or workarounds to correct mistakes that are within the ordinary factory.

364
00:41:41.139 --> 00:41:49.879
John Talburt: And, accountability for quality. And the idea that quality, again, is part of the value proposition

365
00:41:50.009 --> 00:41:56.859
John Talburt: And the quality needs to be seen as value, and it needs to be visible at the highest levels of management.

366
00:42:01.249 --> 00:42:05.159
John Talburt: And then, quality cost.

367
00:42:05.469 --> 00:42:16.569
John Talburt: Now, this is… we're going to see this is in… sort of in contrast, or maybe I shouldn't say contrast, but it's going to be sort of a different way of looking at this idea.

368
00:42:16.979 --> 00:42:21.649
John Talburt: But Feigenbaum is saying, that,

369
00:42:22.409 --> 00:42:29.309
John Talburt: That we need to think about… we need to realize that quality was gonna cause it. We…

370
00:42:29.599 --> 00:42:34.089
John Talburt: To have really quality product, we're gonna have to do some things extra.

371
00:42:34.549 --> 00:42:48.439
John Talburt: And you need to quantify the cost of the quality. Of course, you want it to be as small as possible, but it is going to cost more to get good quality. And I think most people recognize this. They're willing to pay more

372
00:42:48.679 --> 00:42:51.029
John Talburt: For products that are higher quality.

373
00:42:51.349 --> 00:42:55.719
John Talburt: Because it costs more to produce them. But it's interesting, we'll see that

374
00:42:55.849 --> 00:43:03.039
John Talburt: that there's another guy, a guy named Bill Crosby, that,

375
00:43:04.079 --> 00:43:17.219
John Talburt: sort of looked at this in another way, and again, he is an American businessman, and here's what he said. He published a whole book that says, well, quality is free.

376
00:43:17.819 --> 00:43:24.759
John Talburt: So, this is in contrast to Feigenbaum, who said, well, you really need to pay attention and understand the cost

377
00:43:24.889 --> 00:43:26.089
John Talburt: of quality.

378
00:43:26.239 --> 00:43:30.379
John Talburt: And here is Crosby saying, well, but quality is free.

379
00:43:30.999 --> 00:43:36.479
John Talburt: But, again, they're really talking about the same thing. My example is that

380
00:43:37.269 --> 00:43:46.759
John Talburt: that, certainly, Crosby recognized it costs more to ensure quality. It took a little extra time, maybe it took extra workers.

381
00:43:47.079 --> 00:43:50.609
John Talburt: Maybe it took more costly parts.

382
00:43:50.969 --> 00:43:56.949
John Talburt: In information quality, it would be the time, like you're doing in your assignment of

383
00:43:57.049 --> 00:44:13.999
John Talburt: of going through and looking at the data, seeing what the possible defects or anomalies might be, and then, you know, are they important? Do we need to correct them? Do we need to take the time and effort to fix those things? So, there's going to be extra cost

384
00:44:14.329 --> 00:44:20.629
John Talburt: But, his point was, the reason he says that quality is free, is that extra cost

385
00:44:20.999 --> 00:44:29.129
John Talburt: Is more than compensated by The fact that the product that you're building is worth more.

386
00:44:29.409 --> 00:44:42.219
John Talburt: people will… you'll sell more of it, or you can charge more for it, because it's a quality product. And so, in essence, by investing in the quality management part.

387
00:44:42.489 --> 00:44:49.709
John Talburt: That you get that back and more, so that, in essence, the cost of the quality is free.

388
00:44:49.959 --> 00:44:56.549
John Talburt: So, again, it's just sort of another way of looking at what Feigenbaum said. Certainly, there is cost to quality.

389
00:44:56.689 --> 00:44:58.179
John Talburt: But Crosby's…

390
00:44:58.329 --> 00:45:09.489
John Talburt: Argument is that it really doesn't cost anything extra, because you're going to more than make up for it in the price of the product, or the… the…

391
00:45:09.819 --> 00:45:12.949
John Talburt: The quantities that you sell of the product.

392
00:45:14.599 --> 00:45:19.559
John Talburt: And, this is not drift, it's the DIRFT system that he invented.

393
00:45:19.799 --> 00:45:23.229
John Talburt: doing it right the first time, right? So…

394
00:45:23.419 --> 00:45:27.309
John Talburt: That's often said, you know,

395
00:45:27.549 --> 00:45:32.359
John Talburt: How… if you don't have time to do it now, how are you going to find the time to fix it later?

396
00:45:33.589 --> 00:45:40.759
John Talburt: And again, quality, this is the ISO definition. I don't know if it really comes from him or not, but…

397
00:45:41.319 --> 00:45:45.889
John Talburt: It's the idea that quality is conformance or meeting requirements.

398
00:45:49.209 --> 00:45:50.119
John Talburt: And…

399
00:45:50.379 --> 00:46:02.499
John Talburt: the system of quality, the essence of quality, is prevention, right? So, just like you're doing, and you're getting data in, you're going to create some kind of an information product, like a report card, or…

400
00:46:02.629 --> 00:46:05.359
John Talburt: A grade transcript for a grade.

401
00:46:05.639 --> 00:46:07.389
John Talburt: Our grades for transcript.

402
00:46:07.529 --> 00:46:17.019
John Talburt: the, you want to prevent errors as early in the process as possible.

403
00:46:17.379 --> 00:46:20.379
John Talburt: And again, this is what I was talking about earlier.

404
00:46:20.509 --> 00:46:31.739
John Talburt: I don't remember if I told you… Well, I will later, but… this,

405
00:46:33.239 --> 00:46:37.999
John Talburt: The idea that… and you see this a lot even in software development.

406
00:46:38.499 --> 00:46:40.969
John Talburt: Is this idea that it costs

407
00:46:41.699 --> 00:46:47.859
John Talburt: The cost to prevent problems is much smaller than the cost to repair things later, so…

408
00:46:48.219 --> 00:46:55.239
John Talburt: Whatever cost or time that it takes to do things, Correctly, the first time.

409
00:46:55.349 --> 00:47:06.959
John Talburt: is much less than what it takes to have to pull back a product. You see these recalls all the time of having to destroy a lot of food, or recall a lot of,

410
00:47:07.219 --> 00:47:10.339
John Talburt: products, children's…

411
00:47:10.669 --> 00:47:16.899
John Talburt: Seats for cars, car seats, and things like that, that have some kind of defect.

412
00:47:17.169 --> 00:47:25.459
John Talburt: And that the cost becomes 10 hundred times more than the cost of just sort of, as he said, doing it right the first time.

413
00:47:27.369 --> 00:47:36.209
John Talburt: And the performance standard, what we're all looking for is zero defects. However, this is one that's kind of fallen into the…

414
00:47:37.229 --> 00:47:40.919
John Talburt: I mean… If we look at Microsoft, right.

415
00:47:41.229 --> 00:47:46.619
John Talburt: We want it to be as good as it needs to be, and certainly it would be ideal

416
00:47:46.949 --> 00:47:49.579
John Talburt: If we had zero defects, but…

417
00:47:49.689 --> 00:47:55.429
John Talburt: That can be impractical in many situations. It's just almost impossible in many cases

418
00:47:55.689 --> 00:48:00.169
John Talburt: To actually get rid of every error, every anomaly, every problem.

419
00:48:00.379 --> 00:48:03.289
John Talburt: And is it necessary?

420
00:48:03.559 --> 00:48:10.309
John Talburt: You know, how many of these really prevent the thing that you're building from having

421
00:48:11.399 --> 00:48:16.739
John Talburt: Being useful and meeting the requirements of the consumer of that product.

422
00:48:17.129 --> 00:48:22.239
John Talburt: But anyway, it's always good to think about it as a goal of zero.

423
00:48:22.529 --> 00:48:24.549
John Talburt: But it's not always achievable.

424
00:48:27.039 --> 00:48:35.569
John Talburt: And the measure of quality is the price of nonconformance, right? So again, this is where he gets the quality as free, right?

425
00:48:35.729 --> 00:48:42.529
John Talburt: If things don't conform and they cost you because the product is not a meeting, it's,

426
00:48:42.829 --> 00:48:48.019
John Talburt: The requirements of the consumers, Then that tells you what your…

427
00:48:48.499 --> 00:48:56.629
John Talburt: What you should be able to spend, that's a cap, if you will, on how much you can invest in the quality to try to eliminate the non-compliance.

428
00:48:58.399 --> 00:49:07.609
John Talburt: So again, he was very influential, quality pioneer.

429
00:49:08.159 --> 00:49:12.029
John Talburt: Alright, and then we see the shift over to Japan.

430
00:49:13.159 --> 00:49:20.239
John Talburt: Where they were adopted many of these principles of Deming and Shuhart and others

431
00:49:20.369 --> 00:49:23.169
John Talburt: Before it came back to the U.S,

432
00:49:23.599 --> 00:49:26.759
John Talburt: And so they, they're re… they're…

433
00:49:27.129 --> 00:49:32.869
John Talburt: Again, you can see a lot of their words embedded into our current quality processes.

434
00:49:33.559 --> 00:49:40.369
John Talburt: So, Ishikawa… was a Japanese organizational theorist.

435
00:49:41.639 --> 00:49:49.019
John Talburt: And he had this idea of user-friendly quality control. Again, quality control.

436
00:49:49.259 --> 00:49:58.729
John Talburt: Is… is the inspection or the check of conformance of the final product to those,

437
00:49:59.189 --> 00:50:09.469
John Talburt: the customer requirements. So what he means by user-friendly is he is the… the idea is that we, again, need to think about quality from the standpoint of

438
00:50:09.729 --> 00:50:12.109
John Talburt: The finished product that we're building.

439
00:50:12.299 --> 00:50:18.079
John Talburt: And is it friendly to the user? In other words, is it what the user is really wanting?

440
00:50:18.199 --> 00:50:24.719
John Talburt: That's where we should really start. Not with just, we're getting data, let's clean it up the best we can.

441
00:50:25.089 --> 00:50:35.319
John Talburt: And this idea is really entrenched. People, even today, if you go out and talk to somebody about data quality or quality control or anything.

442
00:50:35.449 --> 00:50:41.099
John Talburt: They'll say, yeah, yeah, we get lots of data, and we spend a lot of time scrubbing and cleaning that data.

443
00:50:41.569 --> 00:50:45.079
John Talburt: Well, that, that's probably important.

444
00:50:45.369 --> 00:50:50.839
John Talburt: But it's not where the focus should be. The focus should be on, what am I building?

445
00:50:51.129 --> 00:50:54.019
John Talburt: What is the product or service that I'm building?

446
00:50:54.279 --> 00:51:10.189
John Talburt: From there, then I say, okay, what condition do I need the data to be in? What… first of all, what data do I need? And then what condition do I need it to be in, in order to build this product so that it meets the requirements of the people who are going to use it?

447
00:51:10.879 --> 00:51:21.069
John Talburt: And not just say, well, we're getting data, let's clean it up as much as we can, and we'll throw it into the process, and surely everything will be okay.

448
00:51:21.359 --> 00:51:34.889
John Talburt: So it's sort of the misinterpretation or misquoting of garbage in, garbage out. That's certainly true. You put really bad data into a process, you're bound to get something bad out the other end.

449
00:51:35.009 --> 00:51:37.919
John Talburt: However, the converse is not true.

450
00:51:38.169 --> 00:51:39.739
John Talburt: The converse is that…

451
00:51:39.899 --> 00:51:45.369
John Talburt: If I put in good data, I will necessarily get a good product. That is not true.

452
00:51:45.549 --> 00:51:47.049
John Talburt: It doesn't follow.

453
00:51:47.409 --> 00:51:50.979
John Talburt: So, you have to start with the product.

454
00:51:51.199 --> 00:51:54.819
John Talburt: All you can say from garbage in, garbage out, is that

455
00:51:55.019 --> 00:52:07.009
John Talburt: If you didn't get garbage out, in other words, you got something good out, all you can infer is, well, I didn't put bad data in, because if I'd put the bad data in, I'd certainly have gotten bad product out.

456
00:52:07.339 --> 00:52:11.599
John Talburt: So, we have to be careful how we apply our lodging.

457
00:52:12.739 --> 00:52:23.449
John Talburt: What he's most famous for is the Ishikawa diagram, which nobody probably knows what it is anymore. They always know it by the fishbone name.

458
00:52:23.609 --> 00:52:29.799
John Talburt: So, he was very much… Focused on the idea of root cause analysis.

459
00:52:30.279 --> 00:52:34.589
John Talburt: That if something was going wrong at the product level.

460
00:52:34.749 --> 00:52:39.079
John Talburt: What do we trace it back to? What was causing that?

461
00:52:39.349 --> 00:52:45.179
John Talburt: And so, he was very famous for, using these diagrams to sort of

462
00:52:45.499 --> 00:52:52.359
John Talburt: Inspire or tickle the ideas of… of what should… what are the possible things we should be looking at?

463
00:52:52.929 --> 00:53:10.109
John Talburt: He also, you may have heard of quality circles. This arose again in Japan. This idea of getting people together, sitting down in a circle or around a table, and talking about the process and how, people could,

464
00:53:10.549 --> 00:53:20.769
John Talburt: Get together and understand the entire process and maybe make better improvements to it, rather than just being focused on that part of the process that they were…

465
00:53:21.709 --> 00:53:23.489
John Talburt: Empowered with.

466
00:53:25.059 --> 00:53:31.039
John Talburt: And then emphasize this idea of the internal customer as well, that the customers are not only

467
00:53:31.349 --> 00:53:33.869
John Talburt: The people that you're selling products to.

468
00:53:34.139 --> 00:53:40.959
John Talburt: But there are many cases where your… your customer is internal. This is certainly true for information products.

469
00:53:41.379 --> 00:53:43.779
John Talburt: So, here at the university, we…

470
00:53:44.229 --> 00:53:52.119
John Talburt: we… there's certain ones that go out. For example, I mentioned the fact that we have to report… we're getting ready to report our census data.

471
00:53:52.279 --> 00:53:53.749
John Talburt: For this semester.

472
00:53:54.069 --> 00:53:55.979
John Talburt: to the Department of Education.

473
00:53:56.589 --> 00:53:58.339
John Talburt: It's an external customer.

474
00:53:59.669 --> 00:54:04.189
John Talburt: But we have a lot of internal customers that are using data all the time.

475
00:54:04.509 --> 00:54:07.759
John Talburt: And information products, internal.

476
00:54:08.989 --> 00:54:11.049
John Talburt: So we have to think about those.

477
00:54:11.539 --> 00:54:14.519
John Talburt: A shared vision of what we're trying to accomplish.

478
00:54:15.099 --> 00:54:18.559
John Talburt: And so here's the idea of the fishbone diagram.

479
00:54:19.089 --> 00:54:24.229
John Talburt: And it's this idea that if you…

480
00:54:24.459 --> 00:54:34.109
John Talburt: look at the outcome, what you're trying to… your product or your service that you're trying to build, and it's… there's a defect, so this was an example I found on the internet about

481
00:54:34.379 --> 00:54:36.999
John Talburt: The problem of being late for work, and so…

482
00:54:37.509 --> 00:54:46.929
John Talburt: what could contribute to that? Why? You know, so it could be motivation, people are not motivated, they're tired, they're lazy, dreaming about vacations.

483
00:54:47.099 --> 00:54:49.969
John Talburt: the method, they didn't set the alarm, they didn't…

484
00:54:50.349 --> 00:54:54.199
John Talburt: I don't have a regular time to wake.

485
00:54:54.579 --> 00:54:56.939
John Talburt: Route to work, time management.

486
00:54:57.549 --> 00:55:03.609
John Talburt: kids, partners, family, things that could rent, and so it just goes on. This is just a very simple example.

487
00:55:03.729 --> 00:55:08.169
John Talburt: But the idea is, and there are many of these, depending on certain types of,

488
00:55:08.329 --> 00:55:11.029
John Talburt: Products and services that are being built.

489
00:55:11.139 --> 00:55:17.119
John Talburt: That are already sort of pre-populated, and the whole idea is to get you to think, again.

490
00:55:17.229 --> 00:55:25.599
John Talburt: Back to, this idea that we saw of breakthrough thinking, right?

491
00:55:26.019 --> 00:55:29.409
John Talburt: You need to… sometimes need to…

492
00:55:29.739 --> 00:55:38.009
John Talburt: Go outside of what you're ordinarily thinking about, and so these diagrams help inspire people to think about many different things.

493
00:55:40.899 --> 00:55:48.289
John Talburt: And finally… I talked about, Shaggyo Shingo… Shingo, I'm sorry.

494
00:55:48.899 --> 00:55:56.269
John Talburt: So, Shingo, was a Japanese industrial engineer who really adopted this idea of total quality management.

495
00:55:57.009 --> 00:56:04.669
John Talburt: And he is the major contributor to what's still now used is the Toyota production system.

496
00:56:04.919 --> 00:56:09.809
John Talburt: And it's used by companies worldwide, not only for cars, but other things as well.

497
00:56:10.759 --> 00:56:16.179
John Talburt: he also was a big advocate of just-in-time production.

498
00:56:18.029 --> 00:56:30.889
John Talburt: Which is not to build a lot of things in advance and store them to have them ready in inventory, but to try to build things just in time that they… or just at the time that they needed to be

499
00:56:31.099 --> 00:56:33.889
John Talburt: Produced for the… for the customer.

500
00:56:34.229 --> 00:56:45.919
John Talburt: And because that made the process much more efficient, it didn't mean… it meant you didn't have to store a lot of inventory, either on the parts side or on the product side.

501
00:56:46.159 --> 00:56:54.989
John Talburt: So, how do you… how do you work it so that you get things just in time? And that goes into the idea of lean production, right?

502
00:56:55.189 --> 00:57:00.149
John Talburt: To do as little as possible, spend as little effort as possible.

503
00:57:00.299 --> 00:57:05.199
John Talburt: To produce that, not to have extra labor, extra time, extra parts.

504
00:57:05.389 --> 00:57:10.029
John Talburt: To try to be lean in producing your product or service.

505
00:57:10.889 --> 00:57:14.169
John Talburt: And, anyway, she's very important for that.

506
00:57:14.469 --> 00:57:24.529
John Talburt: And then, kanchi Taguchi, was also very important. He was a Japanese engineer and statistician.

507
00:57:24.759 --> 00:57:29.289
John Talburt: And he's, most famous for the Taguchi loss function.

508
00:57:29.669 --> 00:57:34.689
John Talburt: And it measures financial loss to society from poor quality.

509
00:57:35.049 --> 00:57:42.069
John Talburt: And I tell you, we'll talk about another person that has really built this out.

510
00:57:42.319 --> 00:57:47.599
John Talburt: And extended this idea a lot, as Dr. Tom Redman.

511
00:57:48.149 --> 00:57:51.999
John Talburt: But, the idea that you,

512
00:57:52.399 --> 00:57:59.279
John Talburt: You, you don't just think about you know, the immediate consequence.

513
00:57:59.759 --> 00:58:07.389
John Talburt: Of, of poor quality. In other words, you've got… Poor quality, and

514
00:58:07.519 --> 00:58:14.669
John Talburt: And I'll give you a great example, a real example, that happened not too long ago up in Vermont, I believe.

515
00:58:15.319 --> 00:58:17.499
John Talburt: And that's the idea that,

516
00:58:17.969 --> 00:58:24.169
John Talburt: You know, in the county assessor's office, is where…

517
00:58:24.339 --> 00:58:30.239
John Talburt: They assess the values of property, usually houses and lots and things like that.

518
00:58:30.649 --> 00:58:35.109
John Talburt: And… It has… they collect a lot of information about

519
00:58:35.289 --> 00:58:46.869
John Talburt: You know, how large is the house, how many square feet, and so forth, that they can use to sort of get an idea of how much the value would be, and then how much tax to charge.

520
00:58:47.489 --> 00:59:00.649
John Talburt: Or how to apply the tax rates to that value of the property. But often, this information is shared with real estate people, because they need to be able to sort of estimate the cost of

521
00:59:00.789 --> 00:59:17.889
John Talburt: houses or the prices of houses somebody's selling, they'll say, well, what about the houses that were nearby? Were they similar? Do they have similar square footage, and so forth, and what did they sell for? So, real estate people use that. And so, in this little town in Vermont.

522
00:59:18.369 --> 00:59:24.829
John Talburt: The… one of the real estate agents was allowed to come in and directly access data.

523
00:59:25.319 --> 00:59:29.089
John Talburt: From in the… in the tax assessor's office.

524
00:59:30.549 --> 00:59:33.119
John Talburt: But she made a mistake.

525
00:59:33.369 --> 00:59:38.279
John Talburt: She, and the way this was a system wasn't very sophisticated.

526
00:59:38.519 --> 00:59:42.809
John Talburt: computer system they were using. But the upshot was

527
00:59:43.239 --> 00:59:50.929
John Talburt: She actually, in looking at the data, changed the value of one of the houses that she was looking at.

528
00:59:51.219 --> 00:59:58.639
John Talburt: And to a really, you know, I mean, really big. So it was a house that was only a few hundred thousand dollars.

529
00:59:58.799 --> 01:00:06.829
John Talburt: And suddenly, it became worth a few million dollars just because she injected this error into the system.

530
01:00:07.329 --> 01:00:17.289
John Talburt: Well, of course, when they went to produce the tax bills, one of the tax bills came out for this house that was a few hundred thousand dollars.

531
01:00:17.499 --> 01:00:25.059
John Talburt: And then all of a sudden, the taxes were based on several million dollars. And of course, the guy that owned the house came in and said, look.

532
01:00:25.349 --> 01:00:28.639
John Talburt: This can't be right, and yeah, they went in and they fixed it.

533
01:00:28.929 --> 01:00:44.869
John Talburt: So the point is that Taguchi's loss function says, well, it's not just that. It's not just that it produced one bad text bill, because what also happened was that between the time that the error was made and the error was discovered.

534
01:00:45.149 --> 01:00:53.369
John Talburt: They had already gone out and used the, the values in the system to project the budgets.

535
01:00:53.689 --> 01:01:01.889
John Talburt: for, for the next year for the county. And, you know, what are they gonna do? What roads were they gonna repair, and so forth.

536
01:01:02.079 --> 01:01:20.909
John Talburt: So when they produced that budget, it was much bigger than the previous year, but it wasn't so big that they said, oh, something's wrong. So they just said, hey, man, you know, we've got a lot of extra money this year, let's use it. And they went and they made commitments to contractors, and undertook new projects.

537
01:01:21.039 --> 01:01:27.009
John Talburt: And I already had that underway before they found out that there was an error in the system.

538
01:01:27.429 --> 01:01:44.759
John Talburt: And so even though you could say, well, when you look at the loss function, well, it wasn't much loss to go fix that one tax bill and make that consumer happy. That was a small one. But if you look at the bigger impact of all of that.

539
01:01:44.829 --> 01:01:59.879
John Talburt: It caused a lot of problems because they had already let contracts out for this extra money, and they had to pull those contracts back, had to pay penalties. People thought they were going to get employed, got let go because the

540
01:01:59.909 --> 01:02:04.899
John Talburt: things didn't go through. So, Taguchi's loss function tries to measure

541
01:02:05.519 --> 01:02:11.339
John Talburt: All of the loss that's caused by when these problems happen, not just the immediate.

542
01:02:14.119 --> 01:02:17.399
John Talburt: And he talked about offline quality control.

543
01:02:17.609 --> 01:02:33.229
John Talburt: products and processes insensitive to parameters outside the design engineer's control. In other words, again, this more broad thinking, not only of the overall consequences of poor quality, but what are the things that

544
01:02:33.459 --> 01:02:47.699
John Talburt: That you need to think about that oftentimes design engineers don't really think about when they're thinking more about, you know, how to efficiently build something, and they're not necessarily thinking about

545
01:02:48.629 --> 01:02:52.739
John Talburt: These parameters that are outside the engineer's control.

546
01:02:53.019 --> 01:02:59.149
John Talburt: So having broader thinking, bringing more people into the study process.

547
01:03:01.219 --> 01:03:11.179
John Talburt: And then this idea that now has become enshrined in the idea of digital twins, but a lot of this developed before we had those capabilities.

548
01:03:11.309 --> 01:03:19.889
John Talburt: And that was the idea of trying to design things statistically through, through experiments.

549
01:03:20.069 --> 01:03:25.539
John Talburt: Before you actually commit to building, a final design of a product.

550
01:03:27.279 --> 01:03:33.589
John Talburt: So let me quickly run through some of the, more contemporary.

551
01:03:35.149 --> 01:03:41.569
John Talburt: And as I said, Larry English, who was the founder of Information Impact International and author.

552
01:03:41.979 --> 01:03:51.209
John Talburt: He was responsible for this POSMAD that we talked about, this nonlinear life cycle, this idea that

553
01:03:51.399 --> 01:03:54.009
John Talburt: you know, data…

554
01:03:54.169 --> 01:04:02.949
John Talburt: It has a life cycle in which you plan for it, obtain it, but then once you have it and store it, sure, you continuously apply it and maintain it.

555
01:04:03.169 --> 01:04:05.809
John Talburt: And then, at some point, dispose of it.

556
01:04:06.419 --> 01:04:09.309
John Talburt: And he's best known for his books on,

557
01:04:10.019 --> 01:04:16.259
John Talburt: The, data warehouse is where this all came from. He has two very important books.

558
01:04:16.389 --> 01:04:18.649
John Talburt: Information quality applied.

559
01:04:19.059 --> 01:04:22.969
John Talburt: And improving data warehouse and business information quality.

560
01:04:24.799 --> 01:04:25.989
John Talburt: He's a great fellow.

561
01:04:26.269 --> 01:04:33.529
John Talburt: And then Dr. Wong, who now is the Director of the MIT Chief Data Officer and Information Quality Program.

562
01:04:33.649 --> 01:04:38.469
John Talburt: He's also, was, he just retired as a…

563
01:04:38.899 --> 01:04:41.829
John Talburt: Professor here of information science.

564
01:04:43.669 --> 01:04:56.679
John Talburt: And he… his major contribution was adapting this idea of total quality management and manufacturing to the… to data quality in what he called total data quality man… management.

565
01:04:58.029 --> 01:05:02.449
John Talburt: And he established the International Conference on Information Quality.

566
01:05:02.959 --> 01:05:04.339
John Talburt: And,

567
01:05:04.719 --> 01:05:12.479
John Talburt: instrumental in establishing information qualities and information as an academic discipline. Here, he's one of the founders here.

568
01:05:12.769 --> 01:05:16.749
John Talburt: And he was the executive director for the Institute.

569
01:05:16.879 --> 01:05:18.689
John Talburt: For Chief Data Officers here.

570
01:05:21.839 --> 01:05:30.089
John Talburt: Another, he's very well known, and we'll be studying this over and over in this course, is the Wongstrong Framework for Data Quality and Dimensions.

571
01:05:30.669 --> 01:05:33.309
John Talburt: 16 dimensions we talked about.

572
01:05:34.529 --> 01:05:39.339
John Talburt: And sort of emphasizing what had already been done in manufacturing.

573
01:05:39.789 --> 01:05:49.489
John Talburt: And the idea of managing information as product. In other words, focusing on the product side, and then working backwards, or working outwards, both ways from that.

574
01:05:50.399 --> 01:05:55.859
John Talburt: And, he's probably the most cited author in information quality research.

575
01:05:56.749 --> 01:06:02.229
John Talburt: is currently… is really advocating for this idea of Chief Data Officer.

576
01:06:02.439 --> 01:06:10.259
John Talburt: Several books, Journey to Data Quality, Quality Information and Knowledge, Introduction to Information Quality.

577
01:06:11.189 --> 01:06:12.429
John Talburt: data quality.

578
01:06:14.249 --> 01:06:20.169
John Talburt: Another, is Dr. Tom Redman, who likes to call himself the Datadoc.

579
01:06:20.649 --> 01:06:28.909
John Talburt: And again, going sort of along the lines of, that we talked about a while ago, of Taguchi's loss function.

580
01:06:29.399 --> 01:06:33.869
John Talburt: the understanding of the cost of poor debt. That was one of his things.

581
01:06:33.999 --> 01:06:40.919
John Talburt: And he developed this idea of Rule of 10. It costs 10 times as much to complete a unit of work

582
01:06:41.019 --> 01:06:45.499
John Talburt: When the input data are defective, as it does when the input data are perfect.

583
01:06:46.079 --> 01:06:50.379
John Talburt: In other words, data quality really slows down by order of magnitude.

584
01:06:50.559 --> 01:06:51.649
John Talburt: the process.

585
01:06:52.369 --> 01:06:59.489
John Talburt: And this idea of information change, how information moves, along in,

586
01:06:59.909 --> 01:07:07.919
John Talburt: Through a process, and how oftentimes this is sort of opposite to the way the company thinks about things.

587
01:07:08.219 --> 01:07:08.919
John Talburt: Ruh.

588
01:07:09.209 --> 01:07:13.229
John Talburt: So, data passes from department to department.

589
01:07:13.649 --> 01:07:28.959
John Talburt: And we don't have… and people understand the data when it gets to them and what they need to do with it, but we don't have… many organizations don't have a good understanding of how that data flows and the overall flow of the data.

590
01:07:29.819 --> 01:07:35.399
John Talburt: How it affects the organization, and is affected by the organization.

591
01:07:35.959 --> 01:07:42.019
John Talburt: And he's written several books, I need to bring this up today. Getting in Front on Data, data-driven.

592
01:07:42.619 --> 01:07:45.489
John Talburt: Data quality for the information age.

593
01:07:45.619 --> 01:07:47.629
John Talburt: Data quality, the field guide.

594
01:07:48.229 --> 01:07:53.519
